<div class="sub-container-blog blog-load-event">
  <div class="article-header">
    <div class="article-header-byline-date">
      <div class="article-header-left">
By            <a class="article-header-left-link" href="/healthcareblog/authors/stephanie-thurrott">
              Stephanie Thurrott
            </a>
, Contributing Writer      </div>
      <div class="article-header-right">
          <span class="article-header-date">October 16, 2026</span>
          <time datetime="2026-10-16"></time>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="article-header-eyebrow blog-card-eyebrow">
<a class='article-header-eyebrow-link' href='/healthcareblog/category/teach-me'>Teach Me</a>    </div>
      <h1 class="article-header-article-title">
        Delayed Symptoms After a Head Injury: What to Watch For
      </h1>
  </div>
    <div class="text-card-background-image-v3 " style='background-image: url(/healthcareblog/-/media/images/project/healthcareblog/hero-images/2026/10/delayed-head-injury-symptoms.jpg?h=780&w=1170&hash=83924306DAD2FBB4300CA59849491EB6)'></div>

  <div class="blog-layout-common blog-layout-common--article">
    <div class="blog-layout-common-left">
      <div class="article-content">
        <div class="article-content-whole">
<p>Not all head injuries cause noticeable symptoms right away. Sometimes, symptoms show up later after adrenaline wears off, after you rest or as the brain reacts to the injury.</p>
<p>Delayed symptoms can happen after lots of different types of head injuries, including a fall, sports injury, car crash, bike or scooter crash, workplace injury or a hit to the head or body. Even if you don&rsquo;t pass out, you could still have a concussion or more serious head injury. And symptoms that get worse over time are more worrisome, compared to symptoms that slowly get better.</p>
<p>&ldquo;There is a complex cascade of changes that can develop over time and cause new or worsening symptoms over the first few days,&rdquo; said <a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doctors.bannerhealth.com/provider/ian-crain/461616?unified" target="_blank">Ian Crain</a>, MD, a neurologist specializing in sports injuries with Banner - University Medicine.</p>
<p>If a head injury causes bleeding in the skull, brain bleed symptoms can build up over time and cause symptoms hours to months later.</p>
<h2>Common symptoms after a head injury</h2>
<p>Recognizing mild to moderate traumatic brain injury (TBI) symptoms early is key. <a href="/services/neurology/programs-care/head-injury">Common symptoms</a>&nbsp;you might notice right away include:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Headache or pressure in the head</li>
    <li>Dizziness or balance problems</li>
    <li>Nausea</li>
    <li>Tiredness</li>
    <li>Sensitivity to light or noise</li>
    <li>Blurry vision</li>
    <li>Feeling &ldquo;foggy&rdquo;</li>
    <li>Trouble concentrating</li>
    <li>Memory problems</li>
    <li>Irritability, sadness or anxiety</li>
    <li>Sleep changes</li>
</ul>
<p>You can have a <a href="/services/concussion/symptoms">concussion</a>&nbsp;even if you don&rsquo;t lose consciousness. In fact, only about 10% of people with concussions lose consciousness.</p>
<h2>Delayed symptoms after a head injury: Key concussion warning signs</h2>
<p>Watch for:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Headache that gets worse</li>
    <li>Repeated vomiting</li>
    <li>New or worsening confusion</li>
    <li>Trouble remembering what happened</li>
    <li>Being unusually sleepy or hard to wake</li>
    <li>Behavior changes, irritability or agitation</li>
    <li>Slurred speech</li>
    <li>Dizziness or balance problems that get worse</li>
    <li>Vision changes</li>
    <li>Weakness or numbness</li>
    <li>Trouble walking</li>
    <li>Seizure</li>
    <li>Fluid or blood from the nose or ears</li>
    <li>One pupil that looks larger than the other</li>
    <li>Symptoms that improve and then get worse</li>
</ul>
<p>Delayed symptoms could be caused by the head injury or by mood symptoms. &ldquo;For example, a student, athlete or worker who is worried about losing their spot on the team, losing their friends, falling behind in school or losing income can develop anxiety or depression that can take on a life of their own and worsen over time,&rdquo; Dr. Crain said.</p>
<h2>When to go to the ER: Head injury red flags</h2>
<p><a href="/services/emergency/when-to-go">Go to the ER</a> or call 911 for:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Loss of consciousness, even briefly</li>
    <li>Worsening or severe headache</li>
    <li>Repeated vomiting</li>
    <li>New confusion, agitation or unusual behavior</li>
    <li>Trouble waking up or staying awake</li>
    <li>Seizure</li>
    <li>Slurred speech or trouble talking</li>
    <li>Weakness, numbness or trouble moving one side of the body</li>
    <li>Trouble walking or poor coordination</li>
    <li>Facial drooping</li>
    <li>Vision changes or unequal pupils</li>
    <li>Blood or clear fluid from the nose or ears</li>
    <li>Severe neck pain</li>
    <li>A head injury from a serious fall, car crash, assault or other major trauma</li>
    <li>Any head injury in someone taking blood thinners</li>
    <li>Any head injury with symptoms that are getting worse</li>
</ul>
<h2>Signs of a head injury in children</h2>
<p><a href="/services/concussion/kids">Children</a> may not be able to explain headache, dizziness or confusion clearly. Watch for:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Behavior or personality changes</li>
    <li>Irritability</li>
    <li>Excessive crying</li>
    <li>Unusual sleepiness</li>
    <li>Vomiting</li>
    <li>Poor feeding or refusing to eat</li>
    <li>Loss of balance</li>
    <li>Clumsiness</li>
    <li>Not acting like themselves</li>
    <li>A bulging soft spot, in babies</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not send a child back to sports or play after a suspected concussion until they are cleared. A second head injury before recovery can be dangerous.</p>
<h2>Signs of a head injury in older adults</h2>
<p>Older adults may be at higher risk for serious injury after a fall or head injury and symptoms may be subtle at first. &ldquo;Older adults can have a long window when they seem relatively fine and you might think you are &lsquo;out of the woods&rsquo; before symptoms start,&rdquo; Dr. Crain said.</p>
<p>Do not assume confusion or weakness is just aging. And keep in mind that blood thinners can increase risk after a head injury.</p>
<p>Watch for:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Confusion</li>
    <li>Sleepiness</li>
    <li>New balance problems</li>
    <li>Headache that is getting worse</li>
    <li>Vomiting</li>
    <li>Weakness</li>
    <li>A sudden change in daily function</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to do while watching for symptoms</h2>
<p>After a suspected head injury, you should:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Have someone stay with you, if possible</li>
    <li>Check symptoms over the next 24 to 48 hours or as long as a provider recommends</li>
    <li>Write down when and how the injury happened and how symptoms change</li>
    <li>Avoid alcohol, recreational drugs and activities that put you at risk of another head injury, including sports</li>
    <li>Rest at first, then follow your provider&rsquo;s guidance for returning to school, work, driving, screens, exercise and sports</li>
    <li>Ask a provider what pain medicine is safe, especially if there may be bleeding risk or you take blood thinners</li>
    <li>Don&rsquo;t ignore symptoms that are getting worse</li>
</ul>
<h2>What a provider may check</h2>
<p>When you <a href="/services/concussion/what-to-expect">see a provider for a head injury</a>, they may ask you about:</p>
<ul>
    <li>How the injury happened</li>
    <li>Whether you lost consciousness</li>
    <li>Symptoms and when they started</li>
    <li>Medications, especially blood thinners</li>
    <li>Past concussions or brain injuries</li>
</ul>
<p>They may perform a neurologic exam and check your balance, strength, speech, pupils and memory. They will consider whether you need imaging such as a CT scan or concussion follow-up, neurology, sports medicine or emergency care.&nbsp;</p>
<p>They will recommend a plan for gradually returning to work, school, activity or sports. It can take up to three months to recover from a concussion.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Head injury symptoms can appear some time after an injury, even if you seemed fine at first. A mild headache or tiredness may get better with rest and monitoring but if you have a worsening headache after head injury, repeated vomiting, confusion, behavior changes, weakness, seizure, trouble waking or any symptom that gets worse, you should be checked right away.&nbsp;</p>
<p>If you or someone you care for has symptoms after a head injury, a <a href="/physician-directory">Banner Health provider</a>&nbsp;can help determine <a href="/getcarenow/urgent-or-emergency">what kind of care you need</a>. When symptoms are severe or changing quickly, go to the ER or call 911.</p>
<h2>FAQs about late head injury symptoms</h2>
<h3>Can concussion symptoms show up the next day?</h3>
<p>Yes. Having delayed concussion symptoms is common. Symptoms can appear later, including headache, dizziness, nausea, brain fog, mood changes, sleep changes or sensitivity to light and noise.</p>
<h3>How long should you watch someone after a head injury?</h3>
<p>Watch closely for at least the first 24 to 48 hours and follow provider instructions. Symptoms that worsen or appear later should be checked.</p>
<h3>Is vomiting after a head injury serious?</h3>
<p>Repeated vomiting after a head injury is a warning sign and should be evaluated urgently.</p>
<h3>Can you have a serious head injury without passing out?</h3>
<p>Yes. You do not have to lose consciousness to have a concussion or another serious head injury.</p>
<h3>When should a child go to the ER after hitting their head?</h3>
<p>Go to the ER for repeated vomiting, trouble waking, seizure, worsening headache, confusion, unusual behavior, poor feeding in a baby, trouble walking or symptoms that are getting worse.</p>
<h2>Other useful articles</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.bannerhealth.com/healthcareblog/teach-me/how-to-tell-whether-a-bump-on-the-head-is-serious">How to Tell Whether a Bump on the Head is Serious&nbsp;</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.bannerhealth.com/healthcareblog/teach-me/common-myths-about-concussions">Don&rsquo;t Fall for These 8 Common Myths About Concussions</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.bannerhealth.com/healthcareblog/advise-me/preventing-brain-injuries">8 Tips to Prevent Head and Brain Injuries in Adults and Children</a></li>
</ul>            


